data.content:
These are two separate issues:

## 1. `&&` with `dynamic` and interface operators

C# 15 changed how `&&` resolves when the right operand is `dynamic`. The compiler now resolves `operator true`/`false` **at compile time** on the left operand's static type (`IFilter`). Since `IFilter` defines `operator true`/`false` but **no `operator &`**, the compiler can't synthesize `&&` and fails.

Previously, the entire `&&` was deferred to the runtime binder.

**Fix** — separate the short-circuit from the dynamic call:

```csharp
void Apply(IFilter left, dynamic right)
{
    if (left)
    {
        var combined = left & (IFilter)right;
    }
}
```

Or add `operator &` to `IFilter`:

```csharp
interface IFilter
{
    static IFilter operator &(IFilter x, IFilter y) => x;
    static bool operator true(IFilter x) => true;
    static bool operator false(IFilter x) => false;
}
```

## 2. `ref readonly` lambda — CS0518

CS0518 means a **predefined runtime type is missing**. The `ref readonly` return lambda:

```csharp
var fn = ref readonly int () => ref value;
```

requires the compiler to synthesize an anonymous delegate type with a `ref readonly` return, which depends on well-known types (e.g., `RequiresLocationAttribute`) in the target framework.

This almost always means your **TFM is stale** — the SDK is .NET 11 but the project still targets an older framework. Check your `.csproj`:

```xml
<!-- ❌ old TFM -->
<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

<!-- ✅ correct -->
<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
```

If you can't retarget yet, use a **local function** instead:

```csharp
public static void M()
{
    static ref readonly int Fn() => ref value;
}
```
